[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Managing Test Data with Fixtures and Environment Variables in Cypress"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Effective test automation relies heavily on clean, maintainable, and secure test data. Hardcoding values directly into your test scripts leads to fragile tests that are difficult to update when requirements change. Cypress provides two powerful features to solve this problem: Fixtures for static or structured test data, and Environment Variables for dynamic, sensitive, or environment-specific configurations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Cypress Fixtures"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A fixture is a fixed set of data located in a file, typically stored inside the 'cypress/fixtures' directory. Cypress natively supports JSON files, making them ideal for mocking API responses, user credentials, or form inputs."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// cypress/fixtures/user.json
{
  "username": "test_user_01",
  "email": "test@example.com",
  "role": "admin"
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To load and use this fixture data within a test, you use the cy.fixture() command. This command asynchronously loads the file contents so you can use them in your test assertions or command chains."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"describe('User Profile Test', () => {
  it('Should load user data from a fixture', () => {
    cy.fixture('user').then((user) => {
      cy.get('#username-input').type(user.username);
      cy.get('#email-input').type(user.email);
      cy.get('#role-select').should('have.value', user.role);
    });
  });
});"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Fixtures for API Interception"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Fixtures are frequently combined with cy.intercept() to stub network requests. This ensures your tests run reliably without depending on a live backend state."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"it('Stubs user API response using a fixture', () => {
  cy.intercept('GET', '/api/user', { fixture: 'user' }).as('getUser');

  cy.visit('/dashboard');
  cy.wait('@getUser');

  cy.get('.welcome-message').should('contain', 'test_user_01');
});"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Managing Environment Variables"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"While fixtures handle structured datasets, Environment Variables manage configuration settings that change based on where your tests run—such as base URLs, API keys, or passwords. Cypress offers multiple ways to define environment variables."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using cypress.config.js to define default environment variables."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using a 'cypress.env.json' file for local overrides."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using command-line flags or system environment variables prefixed with CYPRESS_."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Environment Variables in cypress.config.js"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"const { defineConfig } = require('cypress');

module.exports = defineConfig({
  e2e: {
    env: {
      apiBaseUrl: 'https://api.staging.example.com',
      defaultTimeout: 5000,
    },
  },
});"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"To access these variables inside your test code, use the Cypress.env() method. This method acts as both a getter and a setter."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"it('Uses environment variables in a test', () => {
  const apiUrl = Cypress.env('apiBaseUrl');

  cy.request(`${apiUrl}/healthcheck`).then((response) => {
    expect(response.status).to.eq(200);
  });
});"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Sensitive Data Securely"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Never commit sensitive credentials like passwords or API tokens directly into version-controlled files like 'cypress.config.js' or fixture files. Instead, pass them securely via system environment variables in your CI/CD pipeline."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"CYPRESS_userPassword="SuperSecretPassword123" npx cypress run"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can then access this variable in your test securely via Cypress.env('userPassword') without exposing it in your codebase."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Cypress fixtures for structured mock data and environment variables for configuration and secrets, you can build a robust, secure, and highly maintainable test suite capable of running seamlessly across different environments."}]
